## ABSTRACT
It is significant to develop highly efficient electrocatalysts for energy conversion systems. Interface engineering is one of the most feasible approaches to effectively enhance the electrocatalytic activity. Herein, the density functional theory (DFT) calculations predict that the potential barriers of Fe sites at the interface of FeP–CoP heterostructures are lower than that of Fe sites in FeP nanoparticles (NPs), Co sites in CoP NPs, or Co sites in heterostructures. Motivated by the DFT calculation results, FeP–CoP heterostructures have been designed and synthesized by a metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) confined phosphorization method. The FeP–CoP exhibits the lowest overpotential of \(230~\mathsf{m V}\) at the current density of \(10\ \mathsf{m A\cdot c m^{-2}}\) for oxygen evolution reaction (OER), compared with FeP \((470\ m\lor)\) and CoP \((340\ m\lor)\), which outperforms most of transition metal-based catalysts. The Tafel analysis of FeP–CoP heterostructures shows an improved reaction kinetic pathway with the smallest slope of \(90.3\ m\lor\cdot\mathsf{d e c}^{-1}\), as compared to the Tafel slopes of FeP NPs \((137\ m\lor\cdot\mathsf{d e c}^{-1})\) and CoP NPs \((114\ m\lor\cdot\mathsf{d e c}^{-1})\). And the FeP–CoP shows extraordinary long-term stability over \(24\,\mathrm{~h~}\). The excellent activity and long-term stability of FeP–CoP derive from the synergistic effect between FeP and CoP.
## KEYWORDS
electrocatalysts, interface engineering, FeP–CoP heterostructures, oxygen evolution reaction, synergistic effect
## 1 Introduction
Under the global fossil fuel shortage and environmental crisis, the exploration of alternative energy sources is necessary to achieve sustainable development [1]. One of the most promising solutions is electrochemical water splitting to produce hydrogen based on the renewable energy [2]. Many researchers have also reported a tremendous number of electrocatalysts with excellent performance for hydrogen evolution reaction (HER) [3–5]. However, the sluggish kinetics of oxygen evolution reaction (OER) which requires highly efficient and robust electrocatalysts usually hinders the development of water splitting system [6]. Ir/Ru based noble metal catalysts are considered the most active electrocatalysts for OER so far [7]. But the high cost and the scarcity of these catalysts limit their industrial applications. In this regard, high-performance, non-precious, and durable electrocatalysts for OER should be developed to reduce the high overpotentials and accelerate the reaction kinetics, leading to the commercial applications of electrocatalytic water splitting. Recently, earth abundant transition metal (such as Ni, Co, and Fe)-based catalysts have been reported as a promising class of electrocatalysts for oxygen reduction reaction (ORR) [8] and OER, including oxides, hydroxides [9–12], sulfides [13], carbides [14], and nitrides [15]. For example, Tang et al. designed Fe-doped nickel telluride-nickel phosphide electrocatalyst, which exhibited excellent electrocatalytic performance [16].Apart from the composition of catalysts, the surface structure of catalysts has a significant influence on the electrocatalytic performance [17]. Therefore, it is of great significance to regulate the structure and morphology of electrocatalysts which would guide the synthesis of highly efficient electrocatalyst [18]. Interface engineering is one of the most feasible approaches to effectively optimize the electrocatalytic activity [19]. There are six effective interface engineering approache