have been reported as a promising class of electrocatalysts for oxygen reduction reaction (ORR) [8] and OER, including oxides, hydroxides [9–12], sulfides [13], carbides [14], and nitrides [15]. For example, Tang et al. designed Fe-doped nickel telluride-nickel phosphide electrocatalyst, which exhibited excellent electrocatalytic performance [16].
Apart from the composition of catalysts, the surface structure of catalysts has a significant influence on the electrocatalytic performance [17]. Therefore, it is of great significance to regulate the structure and morphology of electrocatalysts which would guide the synthesis of highly efficient electrocatalyst [18]. Interface engineering is one of the most feasible approaches to effectively optimize the electrocatalytic activity [19]. There are six effective interface engineering approaches for rational design of electrocatalysts, including morphology engineering, crystallographic tailoring, defect engineering, catalyst/electrolyte interface regulation, catalyst/substrate interface engineering, and heterostructure design [20, 21]. Great efforts have been made for heterostructure design which has been widely employed to
enhance electrocatalytic performance [22–25]. In particular, the synergistic effect and strong interfacial interactions in heterostructures can improve charge transfer ability and induce an electronic coupling effect, finally optimizing the free energies of reaction steps and boosting the activity [26–30]. Recently, Feng and co-workers suggested that \(\mathrm{MoS}_{2}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{Ni}_{3}\mathrm{S}_{2}\) heterostructure exhibited higher OER catalytic activity than the counterpart [31]. They found that the strong electronic interaction between the two phases \((\mathrm{MoS}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{Ni}_{3}\mathrm{S}_{2})\) has great contribution to the electrocatalytic performance. To date, metal phosphides also have been demonstrated to be promising electrocatalysts for OER. And theoretical calculations have predicted that binary transition metal phosphide shows an exotic performance for electrochemical OER [32]. Therefore, it is of great significance to achieve heterostructure design of binary metal phosphides. However, rational design of heterostructured metal phosphide electrocatalysts with high efficiency is still an ongoing challenge at the practical levels.
In this work, density functional theory (DFT) calculations were conducted to predict the catalytic performance of iron phosphide (FeP), cobalt phosphide (CoP), and Fe–Co phosphide heterostructures (denoted as FeP–CoP) for OER. Benefiting from the interface in heterostructures, both the Fe sites and Co sites in FeP–CoP exhibit lower potential barriers than that of FeP or CoP, in which the Fe sites on the interface show the lowest potential barriers. Therefore, motivated by the results from DFT calculations, FeP–CoP heterostructures have been designed and synthesized by a metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) confined-phosphorization method. The FeP–CoP exhibits the lowest overpotential of \(230\,\mathrm{\mV}\) at the current density of \(10\,\mathrm{\mA{\cdot}c m}^{-2}\), compared with FeP \((470~\mathrm{~mV})\) and CoP \((340~\mathrm{\mV})\), which outperforms most of transition metal-based catalysts. And the FeP–CoP shows extraordinary long-term stability over \(24~\mathrm{h}\). Our findings might open a new avenue for the application of metal phosphides heterostructures in sustainable energy conversion.
## 2 Experimental
## 2.1 Synthesis of MIL-88B nanorods
The MIL-88B nanorods were synthesized according to the previously reported hydrothermal method [33]. Firstly, \(0.16\,\mathrm{~g~}\) F127 was dissolved in \(15~\mathrm{mL}\) deionized water, followed by the addition of \(0.179\ \mathrm{~g~}\ \mathrm{FeCl}_{3}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) under stirring for \(1\ \mathrm{~h~}\). Subsequently, \(0.5\;\mathrm{mL}\) acetic acid was injected and the mixture was stirred for another \(1\ \mathrm{~h~}\). After the addition of \(0.06\ \mathrm{~g~}\ 2\cdot\) aminoterephthalic acid, the reaction mixture was stirred for another \(2\,\mathrm{{h}}\) and subjected to hydrothermal treatment at \(110\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(24\,\mathrm{{h}}\). The dark brown product was collected by centrifugation and washed with ethanol for several times.
## 2.2 Synthesis of MIL-88B@zeolitic imidazolate framework-67 (ZIF-67) dual-MOFs composites
The as-prepared MIL-88B nanorods were dispersed in \(10\,\mathrm{\mL}\) methanol solution of polyvinyl pyrrolidone (PVP) \((0.5\ \mathrm{g},\ M_{\mathrm{W}}=\) 58,000) and stirred at room temperature for \(12\,\mathrm{~h~}\). The PVP-functionalized MIL-88B nanorods were collected by centrifugation, washed by methanol for several times, and redispersed in \(15~\mathrm{mL}\) methanol. To synthesize MIL-88B@ZIF-67 dual-MOFs composites, \(0.3\,\mathrm{~mL~}\) PVP-functionalized MIL-88B nanorods suspension, \(2\;\mathrm{mL}\;100\;\mathrm{mM}\;2\) -methylimidazole (2-MIM) solution, \(5\,\mathrm{\mL\10\mM\Co(NO_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6H_{2}O}\) solution, and \(2.7\,\mathrm{~mL}\) methanol were mixed to react at room temperature for \(24\,\mathrm{~h~}\). Finally, the product was collected by centrifugation, washed with methanol for several times, and dried overnight at \(60\,^{\circ}\!C\).
## 2.3 Synthesis of FeP–CoP heterostructures
The obtained MIL-88B@ZIF-67 dual-MOFs composite was calcinated at \(400\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(1\,\mathrm{~h~}\) in muffle furnace, and further annealed with \(\mathrm{NaH}_{2}\mathrm{PO}_{2}\) at \(350\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(2\,\mathrm{~h~}\) under a flow of \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) in a tube furnace. For comparison, FeP or CoP nanoparticles (NPs) were synthesized by a similar procedure based on MIL-88B or ZIF-67, respectively.
## 2.4 Materials characterization
The morphologies of the samples were characterized by the scanning electron microscopy (SEM, Hitachi S4800) and transmission electron microscopy (TEM, JEOL, JEM-2100). The compositions of the as-synthesized samples were characterized by energy dispersive X-ray spectroscopy (EDX) attached to the SEM High-angle annular dark-field scanning TEM (HAADF-STEM) images and elemental mapping images were obtained by Talos F200x equipped with EDX. Powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) was carried out on a Bruker D8 ADVANCE diffractometer with Cu Kα radiation. X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS, Axis Supra) was used to characterize the valence states of the elements in the samples. The binding energy was calibrated with the measured C 1s peak at \(284.8~\mathrm{eV}\). Elemental compositions were analyzed by a SPECTRO ARCOS spectrometer using inductively coupled plasma optical emission spectrometry (ICP-OES).
## 2.5 Electrochemical measurements
All electrochemical tests were performed on CHI 760E electrochemical workstation using a three-electrode system in \(1.0\,\mathrm{M}\) KOH at room temperature. \(\mathrm{Hg/HgO}\) electrode and graphite rod were employed as reference and counter electrodes, respectively. To prepare the working electrode, \(20~\mathrm{~mg~}\) of synthesized sample was mixed with \(100~\mathrm{\,\upmuL}\) Nafion solution \((5~\mathrm{\wt.\%})\), \(250~\upmu\mathrm{L}\) ethanol, and \(250~\upmu\mathrm{L}\) isopropyl alcohol by ultrasound for \(45\;\mathrm{min}\). Then \(90~\upmu\mathrm{L}\) of the ink was drop-coated on hydrophilic carbon paper \((1\ \mathrm{cm}\times1\ \mathrm{cm})\) with a mass loading of \(3~\mathrm{mg\cdotcm}^{-2}\). Linear sweep voltammetry (LSV) measurements were conducted at a scan rate of \(5\,\mathrm{\mV{\cdot}s^{-1}}\). All the electrochemical measurements were conducted with \(i R\) compensation. All potentials were referenced to the reversible hydrogen electrode (RHE): \(E(\mathrm{RHE})~=~E(\mathrm{Hg/HgO})~+~0.098~+~0.059~\times~\mathrm{pH}\). The electrochemical double-layer capacitance \((C_{\mathrm{dl}})\) was evaluated by cyclic voltammetry (CV) curves measured at non-faradaic potentials range from 0.13 to \(1.44~\mathrm{V}\) (vs. RHE) according to the following equation: \(C_{\mathrm{dl}}{=}\ I_{\mathrm{c}}/\nu\), where \(C_{\mathrm{dl}},\,I_{\mathrm{c}}\) and \(\nu\) are the double-layer capacitance, charging current, and scan rate, respectively. Specifically, the value of \(C_{\mathrm{dl}}\) was estimated by plotting current density variation \((\Delta j\ (j_{\mathrm{a}}\ -\ j_{\mathrm{c}}))\) versus the scan rate at a certain potential, while the half of the slope was \(C_{\mathrm{dl}}\). Electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) measurements were tested from 1 to \(10~\mathrm{Hz}\) at the open-circuit voltage. The long-term stabilities of FeP NPs, CoP NPs, and FeP–CoP heterostructure were performed by chronoamperometry (CA) at the potentials of 1.71, 1.62, and \(1.55\,\mathrm{V}\) (vs. RHE), respectively.
## 2.6 Computational methods
All the DFT calculations were performed by the Vienna ab initio simulation package (VASP) [34] with the projector augmented wave (PAW) method [35]. The exchange-functional was treated using the generalized gradient approximation (GGA) with Perdew–Burke–Emzerhof (PBE) functional [36]. The energy cutoff for the plane wave basis expansion was set to \(400\;\mathrm{eV}\). Partial occupancies of the Kohn–Sham orbitals were allowed using the Gaussian smearing method and a width of \(0.2~\mathrm{eV}\). The Brillouin zone was sampled with the Monkhorst–Pink \(k\) -points of \(4\times6\times4\)
## 3 Results and discussion
## 3.1 DFT calculations
## 3.2 Design and synthesis of heterostructure
Inspired by the DFT theoretical predictions, FeP–CoP heterostructures were synthesized via a two-step method as illustrated in Fig. 2. In a typical synthesis, MIL-88B nanorods were first synthesized through a hydrothermal method [33]. Then the MIL-88B nanorods (Fig. S2 in the ESM) were mixed with cobalt nitrate and 2-MIM at room temperature to produce MIL-88B@ZIF-67. Finally, unique FeP–CoP heterostructures were obtained by chemically converting MIL-88B@ZIF-67 into Fe–Co mixed metal phosphides by thermal treatment with \(\mathrm{NaH}_{2}\mathrm{PO}_{2}\). SEM images (Figs. 3(a) and 3(b)) show the morphology of the as-obtained MIL-88B@ZIF-67 composites. Uniform MIL-88B nanorods are evenly distributed on ZIF-67 polyhedrons, compared with the pure ZIF-67 NPs (Fig. S3(a) in the ESM). The TEM image (Fig. S(c)) unambiguously reveals that the ZIF-67 NPs contain many MIL-88B nanorods which are distributed inside the polyhedrons. The XRD patterns (Fig. S(d)) reveal that the MIL-88B@ZIF-67 exhibits a similar XRD pattern to that of ZIF-67. In addition, EDX spectrum (Fig. S4 in the ESM) shows the existence of Fe and Co elements, evidencing the successful incorporation of MIL-88B nanorods in ZIF-67 NPs. And the elemental mapping
images of the MIL-88B@ZIF-67 (Fig. S5 in the ESM) reveal the uniform distribution of Fe and Co elements. The ratio of MIL-88B nanorods in each MIL-88B@ZIF-67 could be controlled by tuning the amount of MIL-88B nanorods added to the reaction system (Fig. S6 in the ESM).
To achieve sufficient phosphorization, the MIL-88B@ZIF-67 was firstly oxidized at \(400~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(1\,\mathrm{{h}}\) in air (Fig. S7 in the ESM). Subsequently, the FeP–CoP heterostructures were synthesized through phosphorization of the oxidized MIL-88B@ZIF-67 with \(\mathrm{NaH}_{2}\mathrm{PO}_{2}\) at \(350~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(2\,\mathrm{~h~}\) under a \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) atmosphere. After the phosphorization treatment, SEM images (Figs. 4(a) and 4(b)) show the morphology of rhombic dodecahedron is maintained. But the size of rhombic dodecahedron is reduced because of the shrinkage during the thermal treatment. The XRD patterns (Fig. 5(a)) confirm the products consist of FeP (JCPDS no. 39-0809) and CoP (JCPDS no. 29-0497). The Fe/Co atomic ratio is 1:1.6 according to the ICP-OES results (Table S1 in the ESM), which is close to the EDX results (1:1.7) of FeP–CoP (Fig. S8 in the ESM). In addition, phosphorization of the MIL-88B and ZIF-67 was also carried out under the same conditions. In the case of MIL-88B, SEM image (Fig. S9 in the ESM) indicates the structure of nanorods has been destroyed after phosphorization. The XRD patterns (Fig. 5(a)) reveal that the diffraction peaks can be indexed to FeP (JCPDS no. 39-0809). For ZIF-67, Fig. S10 in the ESM shows the morphology can be maintained after phosphorization. According to the XRD patterns (Fig. 5(a)), the obtained product is denoted as CoP.
The structure of FeP–CoP is further examined by TEM (Fig.
4(c)). The rhombic dodecahedral features can be clearly observed. Furthermore, the high-resolution TEM (HRTEM) image (Fig. 4(d)) shows that the (120) crystal plane of FeP (with lattice plane of \(0.250\ \mathrm{nm}\)) couples with the (102) crystal plane of CoP (with lattice plane of \(0.244\,\mathrm{nm}\)), which is consistent with the XRD patterns. And these results confirm the existence of the heterointerface of FeP–CoP, which indicates the successful establishment of heterostructures. The HAADF-STEM image and elemental mapping images suggest the uniform distribution of Co, Fe, and P on the FeP–CoP heterostructure (Figs. 4(e)–4(h)). XPS has been used to measure the elemental compositions and valence states. XPS survey spectrum (Fig. S11 in the ESM) of FeP–CoP confirms the existence of Fe, Co, P, O, and O elements. The O element may arise from the superficial oxidation of FeP–CoP as a result of air contact [40]. And Co–O and Fe–O bonds have also been observed in the XPS spectra of Co 2p and Fe 2p. In the high resolution XPS spectra of Co 2p (Fig. S6b), the peaks at 778.6 (Co \(2\mathrm{p}_{3/2}\)) and 793.7 eV (Co \(2\mathrm{p}_{1/2}\)) are attributed to Co \(2\mathrm{p}_{3/2}\) and \(2\mathrm{p}_{1/2}\) in CoP. The peaks at 707.4 and 720.2 eV in Fe 2p can be assigned to Fe \(2\mathrm{p}_{3/2}\) and \(2\mathrm{p}_{1/2}\) in FeP. The P species with binding energies of 129.7 and 130.6 eV can be assigned to P bonded to Fe or Co (Fig. 5(d)). XPS spectra of CoP (Fig. S12 in the ESM) and FeP (Fig. S13 in the ESM) have also been provided. The binding energy of the Fe \(2\mathrm{p}_{3/2}\) (Fe–P) exhibits a positive shift of \(0.4\;\mathrm{eV}\) than that of pure FeP, suggesting an increased electron density of the Fe in FeP–CoP as compared to pure FeP, which confirms the electron transfer in the heterostructure.
## 3.3 OER catalytic performance
A typical three-electrode system was applied to study the electrocatalytic OER performance of the FeP NPs, CoP NPs, and FeP–CoP heterostructures in \(1.0~\mathrm{M}\) KOH solution. Figure 6(a) shows the polarization curves with \(iR\) correction. The as-prepared FeP–CoP heterostructures catalyst shows the highest OER catalytic performance with the lowest overpotential of \(230~\mathrm{mV}\) at the current density of \(10~\mathrm{mA\cdot cm^{-2}}\), outperforming other samples of FeP with \(470~\mathrm{mV}\), CoP with \(340~\mathrm{mV}\), and commercial \(\mathrm{RuO}_{2}\) with \(260~\mathrm{mV}\), which is also superior to the performance of many other Co-Fe-based electrocatalysts (Table S2 in the ESM). As shown in Fig. 6(b), the kinetic behaviors of the FeP NPs, CoP NPs, and FeP–CoP heterostructures are further explored. The Tafel analysis (Fig. 6(b)) of FeP–CoP heterostructures shows an improved reaction kinetic pathway with the smallest slope of \(90.3~\mathrm{mV\cdot dec^{-1}}\), as compared to the Tafel slopes of FeP NPs \((137~\mathrm{mV\cdot dec^{-1}})\) and CoP NPs \((114~\mathrm{mV\cdot dec^{-1}})\). To further study the charge transfer kinetics, the measurements of EIS were performed
Fig. S14 in the ESM). The Nyquist plots show a semicircle, suggesting the charge transfer process. Among all the samples, the FeP–CoP heterostructures exhibit the smallest charge transfer resistance \((R_{\mathrm{ct}})\), implying the fast electron transfer kinetics.
In addition, the electrochemical surface area area (ECSA) has been adopted to explore the active surface area in OER (Fig. S15 in the ESM). ECSA can be evaluated by \(C_{\mathrm{all}}\). The ECSA value of FeP–CoP heterostructures is determined to \(418.6~\mathrm{mF\cdot cm^{-2}}\), as compared to FeP NPs \((1.08~\mathrm{mF\cdot cm^{-2}})\) and CoP NPs \((290.8~\mathrm{mF\cdot cm^{-2}})\) (Fig. 6(c)), indicating that the FeP–CoP heterostructures have the highest number of exposed active sites.
And stability of the electrocatalysts is also critical in OER performance. The stability is tested by CA method. The FeP–CoP heterostructures display outstanding durability at \(20~\mathrm{mA\cdot cm^{-2}}\) for \(24~\mathrm{h}\) with a negligible current loss (Fig. 6(d)). However, it could be clearly observed that the current densities of FeP NPs and CoP NPs decreased significantly during the CA test (Fig. S16 in the ESM). Apart from electrochemical characterization, the morphology of FeP–CoP heterostructures after the CA test was
characterized by SEM (Fig.S17 in the ESM), indicating the original morphology could be maintained. And the elemental mapping images of the FeP–CoP heterostructures (Fig. S18 in the ESM) also indicate the unchanged elemental compositions. XRD patterns (Fig. S19 in the ESM) of FeP–CoP heterostructures after the CA test also confirm that the crystal structure remains the same after the long-term durability test. Subsequently, XPS measurements (Fig. S20 in the ESM) of the used FeP–CoP heterostructures indicate that Fe and Co cations on the surface are partially oxidized to FeCoOH and CoOOH, which is consistent with the reported mechanism [41, 42]. All these results demonstrate that the FeP–CoP heterostructures show superior electrocatalytic OER performance as well as excellent stability. The higher OER performance of our FeP–CoP heterostructures might be attributed to the following reasons. First, the interface engineering could induce the synergistic effect and strong interfacial interactions in heterostructures which improve charge transfer ability and induce an electronic coupling effect [43]. Second, the unique FeP–CoP heterostructures lead to a large active surface and effective charge transfer. Also, the heterostructures possess much higher robustness which contributes to the higher stability as compared to pure FeP or CoP.
## 4 Conclusions
In summary, motivated by the results from DFT calculations, we have developed a dual-MOF confined-phosphorization approach for the synthesis of FeP–CoP heterostructures. Interestingly, as predicted by DFT calculations, the potential barriers of Fe sites at the interface of heterostructures are lower than that of Fe sites in FeP, Co sites in CoP, or Co sites in FeP–CoP. The electrocatalytic performance of the obtained FeP–CoP heterostructures is in good agreement with the theoretical predictions. The FeP–CoP exhibits the lowest overpotential of \(230\,\mathrm{mV}\) at the current density of \(10\,\mathrm{mA\cdot cm^{-2}}\), compared with FeP \((470\,\mathrm{mV})\) and CoP \((340\,\mathrm{mV})\). Additionally, the FeP–CoP heterostructures show better long-term stability. The enhanced OER performance might be attributed to the interaction between FeP and CoP. This work will be of fundamental significance in the design of heterostructure materials
for water splitting.
